    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. I found that nine escalation handlers were still emitting a plain `Map<String,List<String>>` body (`{\"errors\":[...]}`, `application/json`), while the rest of the codebase (`ValidationExceptionHandler`, `ForbiddenExceptionHandler`, `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`, `GeneralExceptionHandler`) already produced `ProblemDetail` via a shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper.\n\nChanges, all in `.../rest/escalation/`:\n\n- **`ProblemDetails.java`** \u2014 added one small reuse method, `withErrors(ex, status, detail, errors)`, that builds the standard problem detail and preserves the field-level signal the old handlers carried, as an `errors` member. No logic duplicated across handlers.\n- **Converted the nine rejection handlers** to return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` via that helper, keeping their existing HTTP status codes:\n  - 400: `DisposableEmail`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `MissingOwnerFields`\n  - 409: `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n  - 429: `DailyOwnerLimit`\n\nBecause the responses bridge through Spring's message converters, a `ProblemDetail` body is serialized as `application/problem+json` automatically \u2014 the same mechanism the pre-existing problem-detail handlers rely on. The 400s raised by `@Valid` binding already flowed through `ValidationExceptionHandler`, which was compliant, so no change was needed there.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
